"use strict";(self.webpackChunksnippets=self.webpackChunksnippets||[]).push([["5828"],{73377(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"/2026/07/08/","metadata":{"permalink":"/snippets/blog/2026/07/08/","source":"@site/blog/2026-07-08.md","title":"C/C++ Stuff","description":"Some deep dives into how components/tools work, and basic implementations","date":"2026-07-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"c++","permalink":"/snippets/blog/tags/cpp","description":"Anything related to C++. Language features, library usage etc."}],"readingTime":3.95,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Michael Yang","title":"NUS Computer Science","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/michael-yang-68b670204/","page":{"permalink":"/snippets/blog/authors/michaelyang"},"socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/michael-yang-68b670204/","github":"https://github.com/michaelyql"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/michaelyql.png","key":"michael"}],"frontMatter":{"title":"C/C++ Stuff","authors":["michael"],"tags":["cpp"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Greetings!","permalink":"/snippets/blog/2026/06/14/first-blog-post"}},"content":"Some deep dives into how components/tools work, and basic implementations\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Memory allocation (malloc) \\n\\nMalloc is the primary way that systems allocate memory to programs and ensure that programs are isolated from each other and do not overwrite each other\'s memory. It is also mainly used for dynamically allocated (heap) memory.\\n\\nTo simulate the hardware environment and available memory, we can view memory as an array of bytes. We then process requests for memory and implement allocation algorithms to allocate said memory.\\n\\nThe most basic/common algorithms include:\\n- **first fit** (find first address with enough memory to fit the request)\\n    - Downside: may lead to memory wastage\\n- **best fit** (find the smallest slot that is just big enough to fulfil the request)\\n    - Downside: slow, have to iterate through all slots to find the best\\n- **worst fit** (find the worst slot i.e. the largest block available)\\n\\nCommon problems faced when allocating memory include:\\n- **Fragmentation**: there are \'patches\' of contiguous unallocated and unused memory, which if merged together could allow more memory to be allocated\\n\\nSome slightly more optimized strategies are:\\n- **Buddy system allocation** (split memory into blocks of size equal to powers of 2, recursively break down blocks to fulfil requests and merge unused adjacent blocks together to reduce wastage and fragmentation)\\n- **Memory pools** (preallocate large chunk of memory upfront, split into uniformly sized chunks)\\n\\n## Memory pages / frames\\n\\nTypical Linux page size: 4KB\\n- Purpose: it saves a lot of memory and because of TLB (translation lookaside buffer) it doesn\'t impact performance aas much\\n- Page size is enforced at hardware level so you can only support what hardware supports\\n    - Linux uses 8KiB page size on SPARC chips because that\'s what that chip uses\\n    - On Intel, it is 4KiB and, on more recent Intel chips, it also supports 2MiB and, most recently, 1GiB\\n    - Linux can also use 2MiB via the hugepages feature, but it\'s not default because you waste half a page, on average, per process and that can add up\\n    - ARM64, as used for Apple Silicon, apparently can support 4KiB, 16KiB, and 64KiB.\\n\\nBenefit of larger pages (16, 64KB)\\n- \\n\\nAlmost all architectures support a 4kB page size. Some architectures support larger pages (and a few also support smaller pages), but 4kB is a very widespread default.\\n\\nLinux supports two page sizes:\\n\\nNormal-sized pages, which I believe are 4kB by default on all architectures, though some architectures allow other values, e.g. 16kB on ARM64 or 8kB, 16kB or 64kB on IA64. These correspond to the deepest level of descriptors on the MMU (what Linux calls PTE).\\n\\nHuge pages, if compiled in (`CONFIG_HUGETLB_PAGE` is necessary, and `CONFIG_HUGETLBFS` as well for most uses). This corresponds to the second-deepest level of MMU descriptors (what Linux calls PMD) (or at least it usually does, I don\'t know if this holds on all architectures).\\n\\nThe page size is a compromise between memory usage, memory usage and speed.\\n\\n- A larger page size means more waste when a page is partially used, so the system runs out of memory sooner.\\n- A deeper MMU descriptor level means more kernel memory for page tables.\\n- A deeper MMU descriptor level means more time spent in page table traversal.\\n\\nThe gains of larger page sizes are tiny for most applications, whereas the cost is substantial. This is why most systems use only normal-sized pages.\\n\\n\\nUsing huge pages requires mounting the `hugetlbfs` filesystem and `mmap`ping files there.\\n\\nSee: https://unix.stackexchange.com/questions/128213/how-is-page-size-determined-in-virtual-address-space\\n\\n\\n### Virtual memory\\n\\nLinux uses a virtual memory system where all of the addresses are virtual addresses and not physical addresses. These virtual addresses are converted into physical addresses by the processor.\\n\\nTo make this translation easier, virtual and physical memory are divided into pages. Each of these pages is given a unique number; the page frame number.\\n\\nSome page sizes can be 2 KB, 4 KB, etc. For example, a 32-bit bus will have 4 GB address space. \\n\\nYou can find out a system\'s default page size by querying its configuration via the getconf command:\\n```\\n$ getconf PAGE_SIZE\\n4096\\n```\\nThe units are typically in bytes, so the 4096 equates to 4096 bytes or 4kB.\\nThis is hardwired in the Linux kernel\'s source here:\\n\\n```\\n$ more /usr/src/kernels/3.13.9-100.fc19.x86_64/include/asm-generic/page.h\\n...\\n...\\n/* PAGE_SHIFT determines the page size */\\n\\n#define PAGE_SHIFT  12\\n#ifdef __ASSEMBLY__\\n#define PAGE_SIZE   (1 << PAGE_SHIFT)\\n#else\\n#define PAGE_SIZE   (1UL << PAGE_SHIFT)\\n#endif\\n#define PAGE_MASK   (~(PAGE_SIZE-1))\\n```\\n\\nThe hardware (specifically, the MMU, which is part of the CPU) determines what page sizes are possible.\\n\\n## Basic file system \\n\\n- Fragmentation\\n\\n## Thread scheduling algorithms\\n\\n- Round robin, priority based, FCFS\\n\\n## Caching\\n\\n- L1/2/3 cache\\n- Cache locality \\n- Working set\\n\\n## Template Programming \\n\\nWhat does it actually do under the hood?\\n\\n## System calls\\n\\n## Atomics"},{"id":"/2026/06/14/first-blog-post","metadata":{"permalink":"/snippets/blog/2026/06/14/first-blog-post","source":"@site/blog/2026-06-14-first-blog-post.md","title":"Greetings!","description":"My first post","date":"2026-06-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"c++","permalink":"/snippets/blog/tags/cpp","description":"Anything related to C++. Language features, library usage etc."}],"readingTime":0.56,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Michael Yang","title":"NUS Computer Science","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/michael-yang-68b670204/","page":{"permalink":"/snippets/blog/authors/michaelyang"},"socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/michael-yang-68b670204/","github":"https://github.com/michaelyql"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/michaelyql.png","key":"michael"}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Greetings!","authors":["michael"],"tags":["cpp"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"C/C++ Stuff","permalink":"/snippets/blog/2026/07/08/"}},"content":"My first post\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n:::tip\\nWrite tooltips with `:::tip`\\n:::\\n\\n:::danger[Dangerous title]\\nUse `:::danger` for dangerous tooltips\\n:::\\n\\nOther admonitions are `note`, `info`, and `warning`.\\n\\nMark out code blocks:\\n```python\\nprint(\\"Hello world\\")\\n```\\nAdd code block titles with `title=\\"...\\"`\\n```python title=\\"src/goodbye-world.py\\"\\nprint(\\"Goodbye world\\")\\n```\\n\\nHighlight lines with `highlight-next-line`, `highlight-start`, and `highlight-end`, show line numbers with `showLineNumbers=start` (default to start from 1)\\n```cpp showLineNumbers\\nint main() {\\n    // highlight-next-line\\n    cout << \\"Hello world\\\\n\\" << \'\\\\n\';\\n}\\n```\\n\\nAdd links with `[text to display](./relative-path)`\\n\\nAdd images with `![fallback text](./path-to-img.png)`\\n\\nWrite math using `$$` or blocks with `math` as the language. For example:\\n$$\\na + b = c\\n$$\\n$\\\\pi$"}]}}')}}]);